+Replacing fields
+----------------
+
+Use :meth:`Requirement.__replace__` to create a new requirement with selected
+fields changed. All arguments are keyword-only; omitted fields are preserved.
+On Python 3.13 and later, :func:`copy.replace` can also be used.
+
+.. doctest::
+
+    >>> req = Requirement('requests[security]>=2.0; python_version >= "3"')
+    >>> req.__replace__(name="flask")
+    <Requirement('flask[security]>=2.0; python_version >= "3"')>
+    >>> from packaging.specifiers import SpecifierSet
+    >>> req.__replace__(specifier=SpecifierSet(">=3.0"), marker=None)
+    <Requirement('requests[security]>=3.0')>
+
+The supported fields are ``name``, ``url``, ``extras``, ``specifier``, and
+``marker``, using the same types as the corresponding attributes. Set ``url``
+or ``marker`` to ``None`` to remove them, or use an empty set or
+:class:`~packaging.specifiers.SpecifierSet` to remove extras or version
+constraints. A URL and a nonempty specifier cannot be combined.
+
+Values are validated and normalized by parsing the resulting requirement.
+Invalid values, including values that would parse into different fields, raise
+:class:`InvalidRequirement`. The result has independent mutable fields and
+preserves explicit prerelease settings on the specifier set and its members.
+Subclasses are constructed through their own initializer.
+
+.. versionadded:: 26.4
+
 Reference
 ---------
 
